WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:05.950
Good afternoon, everyone, and thank you for
joining us today.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:11.560
My name is Wayne Matten and I am joined today
by Yumi Jin, we call him Jimmy.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:16.710
It is important that Jimmy is here because
he is the developer of the GRAF software.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:21.720
He will be answering questions in the questions
pod.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:27.119
If you have some questions that you want to
ask of me or Jimmy, if it is about the GRAF

00:00:27.119 --> 00:00:29.690
software, I recommend emailing Jimmy.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:31.910
Our emails are there.

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:33.510
matten@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov
jinyu@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.579
This is what the plan is for today.

00:00:35.579 --> 00:00:39.780
I will introduce GRAF briefly.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:44.199
Talk about why it was created and how it works
at a superficial level.

00:00:44.199 --> 00:00:48.519
And mostly do some demonstrations of how to
use GRAF.

00:00:48.519 --> 00:00:54.889
The two main functions of GRAF are to identify
kits and closely related samples in your data.

00:00:54.889 --> 00:01:00.940
And a relatively new feature is the ability
to look at populations and determine, estimate

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.600
subject ancestries within those data sets.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:06.300
That is the basic plan today.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.210
Everything will be in slides.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:16.870
At the end I may go to the webpages and show
you how some of those works based on a CGI

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:23.420
that Jimmy and folk set dbGaP have developed.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:25.190
Why was GRAF created?

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:30.310
I mentioned there are two main components
but the original one was essentially created

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:36.430
to help deal with the dbGaP data in the sense
that dbGaP is a huge database.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.800
There are hundreds of studies now.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.590
Incorporating well over 1 million individuals.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:50.990
As you know, must GWAS, Genome Wide Association
Studies make assumptions that individuals

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:54.070
in the study are not closely related.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:02:01.720
One function of GRAF is to add as a curation
tool to pick out duplicates and very close

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:02.720
relatives.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:07.480
Identify those and deal with those as you
see fit for your analyses.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:14.030
Part of that process and I put these terms
here because they may come up in later slides,

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:22.430
the subject sample mapping files and pedigree
files are pretty commonly known about files.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:26.989
Those get used in that first step in looking
at relatedness.

00:02:26.989 --> 00:02:33.519
The second function we call GRAF-pop, or population,
was added fairly recently.

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:38.640
This function helps estimate subject ancestries.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:44.829
Basically it deals with the problem of the
assumption of having individuals that are

00:02:44.829 --> 00:02:47.770
not related, you can pick those out.

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:57.540
And it also deals with the issue of using
different genotyping platforms.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:03.189
It normalizes that problem as well.

00:03:03.189 --> 00:03:09.540
As part of the population addition, we have
started adding links on dbGaP pages.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:13.800
This is a screenshot of the dbGaP Advanced
search page.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:19.170
There is a link there called population graph.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:25.570
If you were to open this first ARED study,
there would be a link within that first page

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:29.669
of the study that is called ancestry components.

00:03:29.669 --> 00:03:32.579
Those will both take you to the same graph.

00:03:32.579 --> 00:03:35.200
Which would look something like this.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:41.150
This is a very simplified data set here with
mostly Europeans.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:46.269
But this is just a teaser of what we will
talk about later when we start talking about

00:03:46.269 --> 00:03:48.809
the GRAF population function.

00:03:48.809 --> 00:03:54.349
Those graphs are appearing more of the studies.

00:03:54.349 --> 00:03:59.059
Very briefly how does GRAF work ? It will
be at a superficial level.

00:03:59.059 --> 00:04:05.790
We have detailed questions about this, there
is a publication in PLOS one that came out

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:07.109
last year.

00:04:07.109 --> 00:04:10.860
The link for that is on many of the pages
I will show you.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:14.150
Including the software page where you can
download the software.

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:16.280
I highly recommend that paper.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:20.019
To give you some fundamental background.

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:23.510
Mostly to introduce terms that will be on
some slides.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:30.850
A key component here in identifying the duplicates
and close related subjects is a set of preselected

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:33.200
fingerprint SNPs.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:34.870
Right now there are about 10,000.

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:38.610
I think there may be plans to add to that.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:42.490
Jimmy is nodding his head, yes.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:44.650
Those are very carefully selected.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:49.740
They represent many of the common genotyping
platforms.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:55.980
They were selected to be bi-allelic and have
relatively high allele frequencies.

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:01.340
They were selected to avoid linkage disequilibrium
as much is possible.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.500
They are well separated.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:08.600
They happen to cover, well represent the 22
autosomes.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:13.190
Also there is no strand information needed
in the data set.

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:15.370
There are no complementary alleles.

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:17.430
No A/T or G/C pairs.

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:21.970
You don't have to worry about strandedness
in your data.

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:23.870
That is a key component.

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:26.000
There are two statistical metrics.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.020
All genotypic mismatch rate.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:30.370
AGMR.

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:33.680
And the homozygous genotype mismatch rate.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.430
Those definitions are fairly self-explanatory
there.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:41.090
You will be seeing those on some of the graphs
we look at.

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:45.370
That is for the the relatedness this aspect
of GRAF-pop.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:49.550
The population component is a bit more complicated.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:51.010
And very cool.

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:56.420
I will not be able to go into the math here
because I don't understand it that well.

00:05:56.420 --> 00:06:02.440
At a superficial level, these subjects get
clustered using genetic distances from some

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.590
reference populations.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:12.020
And those clusters get projected onto a plane
and based on their barycentric coordinates

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:17.380
you can look at the position of those clusters
and get some idea as to where they fall relative

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:20.210
to the reference populations.

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:25.980
Also, part of the process is to develop these
statistical metrics, genetic distance one

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:26.980
through four.

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:31.730
We will look at how plotting those in different
ways will give you different views of these

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:34.430
planes and almost rotate the planes.

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:39.490
We will be looking at those plots in a little
bit of detail as we go along.

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:43.950
Okay, one of the main questions is how will
it help you?

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:46.480
You can download the GRAF-pop software.

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:52.570
And run that locally on either your own data
sets or the dbGaP data or other data.

00:06:52.570 --> 00:07:00.141
One possible use case is in addition to trying
to pick out some errors in your data, you

00:07:00.141 --> 00:07:06.000
can also answer questions like, are the subjects
that I'm wanting to add to a new project,

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.540
have they been represented in other studies?

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:12.170
You want to be aware of that.

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:19.470
And then another good use is if you are submitting
data to dbGaP, the curators can now send you

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:28.230
a URL based on the CGI that Jimmy and others
at dbGaP developed.

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:31.310
And these URLs will show you the plots themselves.

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:38.260
So you can get some checks on the data you
are submitting before it is finalized and

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:40.350
that is very helpful in the process.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:47.590
Those links will be similar to some of the
ones that are showing up on the web pages.

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:50.050
Where can you get the GRAF package?

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:57.080
If you do an Internet search for dbGaP software,
one of the first pages will be this page of

00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:58.080
software.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:02.430
You can download the zip file there.

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:09.790
Compiled only for GNU/Linux, a link to the
paper I was talking that to give you more

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:11.600
details until how GRAF-pop works.

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:18.960
Are there any plans to compile this on other
platforms or make the source available?

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.170
No immediate plans to do that.

00:08:21.170 --> 00:08:23.170
We will see what happens in the future.

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:25.860
I will give you a quick look at what is in
that package.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:28.940
I will not run anything on the command line
today.

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:31.400
This will give you an idea.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:34.440
There are two very good documents.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:38.560
The readme on top is enough to get you going.

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:41.840
Running this on the command line is very simple.

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:48.610
The pop documentation was added later because
that describes the population graphing function.

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:54.060
Then you have four programs in there/ Two
Perl scripts to do the plotting.

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:56.670
And two types of graph programs.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:02.880
If you wanted to run GRAF-pop, -pop is an
option on the graph commandline.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:08.510
There is the set of fingerprinting SNPs.

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:12.500
An example commandline could be as simple
as this, graf -plink affy_hapmap -out aff_hapmap_rels.txt

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:17.420
with the option plink, most of you are probably
familiar with what a plink set is.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:22.240
It includes these three file types, .bed,
.bim, .fam.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:29.850
You specify the plink set and simply output
a table of those relationships.

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:35.630
There are other options if you need to have
a sample subject mapping file.

00:09:35.630 --> 00:09:38.680
Because your sample and subject IDs may differ.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:43.660
You can add that or you can add a pedigree
file whatever your data set requires.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:51.279
After you run that first GRAF run to get that
output file, you can plot that using one of

00:09:51.279 --> 00:09:52.490
the Perl scripts.

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:53.840
Plot graph Perl.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:59.910
It put that output from the previous file
and that will create a PNG file.

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:05.500
Here's an example of what the PNG out might
look like.

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:07.440
That is enough for the commandline.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:14.210
I want to jump into some results to explain
what some of these plots mean.

00:10:14.210 --> 00:10:18.940
The top row represents the relatedness functions.

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:23.500
And after looking at a few of those we will
look at some of the population scatterplots

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:25.320
as well.

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:31.550
In the simplest case here, we are plotting
on the X axis the all genotype mismatch rate

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:35.120
against some number of pairs on the Y axis.

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:39.890
In this case we are looking at duplicate samples
and monozygotic is twins.

00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:45.370
In other words duplicates.

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:48.740
You can adjust the number of pairs.

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:54.200
If we set that number of pairs to 50, we basically
zoom in on the graph.

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:58.010
And so you can start to pick out some things
that would be good to know about.

00:10:58.010 --> 00:11:05.720
For example, the gray at the top of the identical
pairs shows you that there are some that are

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:07.600
not reported in this data set.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:14.540
I a should also point out the vertical lines
are sort of rough estimates of where identical

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:15.660
would fall out.

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:17.820
Where full siblings would fall out.

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:20.420
PO stands for parent offspring.

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:25.220
D2 D3 are 2nd degree and 3rd degree relatives.

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:27.450
And Un is unrelated.

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:32.510
In this data set it looks like there is some
reported duplicated pairs.

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:41.520
That might actually be first-degree relatives
based on this all genotype mismatch rate.

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:45.930
You should definitely be well below 25% if
you want to identify something as identical

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:50.230
or monozygotic twins.

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:54.070
We also see out here a couple that were reported
as monozygotic twins.

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:56.430
But the rate is very high.

00:11:56.430 --> 00:11:57.470
Around 55%.

00:11:57.470 --> 00:11:59.720
Which would indicate in fact they are probably
unrelated.

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:04.540
That is the sort of checks this plot can give
you.

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:08.899
Again, that was the AGMR against the number
of pairs.

00:12:08.899 --> 00:12:15.840
You can also plot the HGMR on the X axis.

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:18.070
And the number of pairs on the other axis.

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:25.420
Here a looking at full sibling, parent offspring,
second-degree, third-degree, degree of relatedness

00:12:25.420 --> 00:12:29.220
is decreasing as you go to the right.

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:35.160
And if we look at that in some detail, we
see that some of these parent offspring pairs

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:38.770
are not reported.

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:43.029
Same for the full sibling pairs.

00:12:43.029 --> 00:12:46.490
So the gray are they not reported.

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:51.980
It looks like there is some second-degree
relatives not reported.

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:57.680
And also in the unrelated, it looks like they
thought there were monozygotic twins or full

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:01.500
sibling pairs, but they are actually probably
unrelated.

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:05.589
We can zoom in on that to see it better.

00:13:05.589 --> 00:13:10.640
So you see the color coding for the monozygotic
twins.

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:14.890
Probably unrelated based on the GRAF calculations.

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:25.339
If you plot the HGMR versus the AGMR , with
AGMR on the y-axis, you get this scatterplot.

00:13:25.339 --> 00:13:28.020
Here we have parent offspring.

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:31.820
Full sibling, second 2nd degree and 3rd degree
relatives.

00:13:31.820 --> 00:13:39.450
These elipses here represent where 95% calculated
would occur in those particular types.

00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:42.990
You see the full sibling is pretty good.

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:48.399
But one good reason to do this is you can
start to pick out outliers easier in this

00:13:48.399 --> 00:13:51.459
type of plot.

00:13:51.459 --> 00:13:55.610
Let's move on to the GRAF population examples.

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:58.710
This is how you can infer subject ancestry.

00:13:58.710 --> 00:14:01.700
As an example we will look at some real data
here.

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:10.080
Here by default what this plot is on the X
axis, the genetic distance one, on the y-axis

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:11.180
GD two.

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:15.020
Here is the plane I was talking about.

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:18.450
On this vertice is European.

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:20.290
African in lower left.

00:14:20.290 --> 00:14:22.440
East Asian in lower right.

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:28.670
These are color-coded by the reported ancestry.

00:14:28.670 --> 00:14:32.000
And then the positions are what are calculated
by GRAF.

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:35.940
You can see there are some mixtures in here.

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:42.790
For example, in this region here, self-reported
Hispanics and Asian Indians are overlapped

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:43.839
in this area.

00:14:43.839 --> 00:14:50.770
And on this axis, self-reported Hispanics
overlap with African backgrounds.

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:57.520
There is a pretty clear cluster of Asian Indians
here.

00:14:57.520 --> 00:15:00.779
This is how it looks in that particular plane.

00:15:00.779 --> 00:15:05.740
But if you then change the plot, I will show
you how to do this on the webpages, you put

00:15:05.740 --> 00:15:12.190
GD4 on the X axis, then you begin to separate
out some of these populations.

00:15:12.190 --> 00:15:18.850
For example, Hispanics are nicely separated
now from the South Asians.

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:23.779
Also, it separates these two Asian populations
a little better.

00:15:23.779 --> 00:15:27.430
That is the real advantage of going to that
particular type of plot.

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:29.480
One more example of that.

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:36.410
Here, we have a mixture of South Asians with
Hispanics.

00:15:36.410 --> 00:15:39.780
This is the GD one versus GD two.

00:15:39.780 --> 00:15:45.290
And if we plot that against GD four, you begin
to get a nice separation of those.

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:50.410
Again, another good example of plotting and
using these different metrics and how they

00:15:50.410 --> 00:15:53.380
help you tease apart the ancestries.

00:15:53.380 --> 00:16:00.590
This let's go over to the webpage and see
if I can demonstrate some of this.

00:16:00.590 --> 00:16:05.680
I'll go over to the advanced search page.

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:12.779
Here is an example of the population graph
link.

00:16:12.779 --> 00:16:14.380
Click on that.

00:16:14.380 --> 00:16:17.980
Then you see the plot we were looking at before.

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:24.740
You also have some options over here to change
what you are looking at.

00:16:24.740 --> 00:16:28.959
Plot GD 4 and that type of thing.

00:16:28.959 --> 00:16:33.800
What I wanted to show was so here you have
options to change the plotting.

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:39.790
Right now this is the HGMR plot against the
number of pairs we saw earlier.

00:16:39.790 --> 00:16:46.860
All you have to do to get this scatterplot
plot is click on the HGMR plus AGMR button

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:52.040
and that will re plot that.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:17:03.420
In terms of the population graphs, here is
one where we are just looking at the plane.

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:05.929
We have some mixtures here.

00:17:05.929 --> 00:17:11.920
Maybe you can tease that out better by plotting
with GD 4 on the Y axis.

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:16.660
Check that and it redraws that.

00:17:16.660 --> 00:17:25.050
You can do things -- it depends on your access
to the data as to how many options you have,

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:27.800
on the URLs that the curator he might send
you.

00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:30.900
You have access to that data so you will get
more options.

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:33.730
If you don't have the axis, you are more limited
